          "title": "2.4 Intonation and Sentence Stress",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6837561/pexels-photo-6837561.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pts0A7Kb_Wg",
          "content": "2.4 Intonation and Sentence Stress\n\nWhile word stress deals with prominence within individual words, sentence stress and intonation relate to prominence and pitch patterns across entire phrases, clauses, and sentences.\n\nSentence Stress (Prominence or Tonicity):\n\nIn an utterance, certain words carry more stress or emphasis than others. These stressed words are usually content words (nouns, main verbs, adjectives, adverbs) which carry the most meaning. Function words (articles, prepositions, auxiliary verbs, pronouns, conjunctions) are typically unstressed, unless specifically emphasized for contrast.\n\nThe most prominent stress in a tone unit (a segment of speech with a complete intonation pattern) usually falls on the nucleus or tonic syllable, which is often the last stressed content word in the tone unit.\n\nFunction: Highlights new or important information, creates rhythm, and can affect meaning or focus.\n\nExample (nucleus underlined):\n\n\"I want a cup of <u>tea</u>.\" (Neutral focus on tea)\n\n\"<u>I</u> want a cup of tea.\" (Not someone else)\n\n\"I <u>want</u> a cup of tea.\" (Really desire it)\n\n\"I want a <u>cup</u> of tea.\" (Not a mug)\n\nIntonation (Pitch Melody):\n\nRefers to the pattern of pitch changes (rise and fall of the voice) over an utterance. Intonation plays a crucial role in conveying meaning beyond the words themselves.\n\nFunctions of Intonation:\n\nGrammatical Function: Can distinguish between types of sentences (e.g., statement vs. question). A rising intonation often signals a yes/no question, while a falling intonation often signals a statement.\n\nAttitudinal Function: Conveys the speaker's attitude, emotion, or mood (e.g., surprise, certainty, doubt, sarcasm, politeness).\n\nDiscourse Function (Organizational): Helps to segment speech into meaningful units (tone units), indicates an intention to continue speaking or to yield the floor, highlights new versus given information, and signals topic shifts.\n\nAccentual Function: Works with sentence stress to make certain words prominent (the nucleus carries the main pitch movement).\n\nCommon Intonation Contours (Tunes) in English:\n\nFalling Tone (↘): The pitch falls on or from the nucleus, usually towards the end of the tone unit.\n\nTypically used for: Statements, commands, wh-questions (what, where, when, why, who, how), exclamations, indicating finality or completion.\n\nExample: \"She lives in London.↘\" \"What time is it?↘\" \"Sit down.↘\"\n\nRising Tone (↗): The pitch rises on or from the nucleus, usually towards the end of the tone unit.\n\nTypically used for: Yes/no questions, expressions of doubt or uncertainty, requests for repetition, polite requests, listing items (except the last one in a list which may fall).\n\nExample: \"Are you ready?↗\" \"You're sure?↗\"\n\nFall-Rise Tone ( N or ͡ ): The pitch falls and then rises on or from the nucleus.\n\nOften conveys: Uncertainty, reservation, hesitation, polite disagreement or correction, warning, implication, contrast.\n\nExample: \"Well N I <u>suppose</u> so.\" (implying doubt) \"It's N <u>possible</u>.\"\n\nRise-Fall Tone (ˆ): The pitch rises and then falls on or from the nucleus.\n\nOften conveys: Strong feelings like surprise, excitement, sarcasm, strong approval, or disapproval.\n\nExample: \"That's ˆ<u>fan</u>tastic!\" \"You ˆ<u>can't</u> be serious!\"\n\nThe choice of intonation contour is highly context-dependent and interacts with sentence stress and other prosodic features (like rhythm and tempo) to convey meaning. Mastering intonation is vital for fluent, natural, and effective spoken communication in English.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Examples of sentences with different sentence stress placements to show shift in focus. Simple line diagrams showing falling, rising, fall-rise, and rise-fall intonation contours over example sentences.)",
