          "title": "5.4 Adjectives and Adverbs: Forms, Comparison, Functions",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/355952/pexels-photo-355952.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CzHotHaXGk0",
          "content": "5.4 Adjectives and Adverbs: Forms, Comparison, Functions\n\nAdjective (Adj): A word that modifies (describes or qualifies) a noun or pronoun. Adjectives provide more information about the qualities or characteristics of nouns/pronouns.\n\nFunctions:\n\nAttributive: Occur before the noun they modify (e.g., \"a <u>red</u> car,\" \"a <u>happy</u> child\").\n\nPredicative: Follow a linking verb and describe the subject (e.g., \"The car is <u>red</u>.\" \"The child seems <u>happy</u>.\").\n\nTypes/Categories:\n\nDescriptive (Qualitative): hot, big, beautiful, interesting.\n\nQuantitative: many, few, some, several, two.\n\nDemonstrative: this, that, these, those (when modifying nouns).\n\nPossessive: my, your, his, her, its, our, their (when modifying nouns).\n\nInterrogative: which, what, whose (when modifying nouns in questions).\n\nProper: English, Nigerian, Shakespearean.\n\nOrder of Adjectives: When multiple adjectives modify a noun, they often follow a preferred order (e.g., opinion > size > age > shape > color > origin > material > purpose + Noun: \"a lovely big old round brown German wooden serving table\").\n\nComparison of Adjectives: Adjectives can show degrees of comparison.\n\nPositive Degree: The base form (e.g., tall, beautiful).\n\nComparative Degree: Compares two items. Formed by adding -er (for short adjectives) or using \"more\" (for longer adjectives).\n\nExamples: tall<u>er</u>, <u>more</u> beautiful.\n\nSuperlative Degree: Compares three or more items, or indicates the highest degree. Formed by adding -est (short adjectives) or using \"most\" (longer adjectives). Usually preceded by \"the.\"\n\nExamples: the tall<u>est</u>, the <u>most</u> beautiful.\n\nIrregular comparisons: good → better → best; bad → worse → worst; many/much → more → most; little → less → least.\n\nAdverb (Adv): A word that modifies a verb, an adjective, or another adverb. Adverbs provide information about manner, place, time, frequency, degree, etc.\n\nMany adverbs are formed by adding -ly to an adjective (e.g., \"quick\" → \"quick<u>ly</u>,\" \"beautiful\" → \"beautiful<u>ly</u>\"). But not all adverbs end in -ly (e.g., fast, well, very, often, here, now), and not all words ending in -ly are adverbs (e.g., \"friendly\" - adjective).\n\nTypes of Adverbs:\n\nAdverbs of Manner: How an action is done (e.g., slowly, carefully, well, fast).\n\nAdverbs of Place: Where an action occurs (e.g., here, there, everywhere, nearby, up).\n\nAdverbs of Time: When or for how long an action occurs (e.g., now, then, yesterday, soon, already, still).\n\nAdverbs of Frequency: How often an action occurs (e.g., always, often, sometimes, never, usually, rarely).\n\nAdverbs of Degree (Intensifiers): How much or to what extent (e.g., very, extremely, too, quite, rather, so, enough). Often modify adjectives or other adverbs.\n\nConjunctive Adverbs: Connect independent clauses or sentences and show a logical relationship (e.g., however, therefore, moreover, consequently, thus, nevertheless). Often set off by commas.\n\nComparison of Adverbs: Similar to adjectives, some adverbs (especially those of manner that don't end in -ly, or one-syllable -ly adverbs) can be compared using -er/-est or \"more\"/\"most.\"\n\nExamples: fast → faster → fastest; early → earlier → earliest.\n\nMost adverbs ending in -ly use \"more\" and \"most\": \"more quickly,\" \"most carefully.\"\n\nIrregular comparisons: well → better → best; badly → worse → worst.\n\nPosition of Adverbs: Can vary, but common positions include:\n\nAfter the verb (if intransitive) or object (if transitive): \"She sings <u>beautifully</u>.\" \"He read the book <u>quickly</u>.\"\n\nBefore the adjective or adverb they modify: \"<u>very</u> tall,\" \"<u>extremely</u> carefully.\"\n\nAdverbs of frequency often go before the main verb (but after \"be\"): \"She <u>often</u> visits.\" \"He is <u>always</u> late.\"",
          "mcq": {
            "question": "In the sentence 'She sings beautifully,' the word 'beautifully' is an...",
            "options": ["Adjective modifying 'she'", "Adverb modifying 'sings'", "Adverb modifying 'she'", "Adjective modifying 'sings'"],
            "correctAnswer": "Adverb modifying 'sings'",
            "explanation": "Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. Here, 'beautifully' is an adverb of manner describing how she performs the action of the verb 'sings'."
          }
