          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ro6HGADrUZc",
          "content": "8.4 Writing Advertisements, Notices, Invitations\n\nThese are forms of public communication designed for specific, often brief, interactions.\n1.  **Advertisements (Ads):**\n    *   **Purpose:** To persuade an audience to buy a product, use a service, support a cause, or adopt an idea.\n    *   **Key Elements:**\n        *   Headline: Catches attention, often a slogan or a question.\n        *   Body Copy: Provides information, highlights benefits, creates desire.\n        *   Visuals (Images/Graphics): Attract attention, reinforce message.\n        *   Call to Action: Tells the audience what to do next (e.g., \"Buy now!\", \"Visit our website,\" \"Call today\").\n        *   Logo/Brand Name.\n    *   **Language:** Persuasive, evocative, often uses rhetorical devices (e.g., repetition, rhetorical questions, superlatives, emotional appeals), positive language, simple and direct sentences. Targets a specific audience. AIDA model often used (Attention, Interest, Desire, Action).\n\n2.  **Notices:**\n    *   **Purpose:** To provide important information to a specific group of people or the public about an event, change, rule, or warning.\n    *   **Key Elements:**\n        *   Clear Heading (e.g., \"NOTICE,\" or specific title).\n        *   Issuing Authority/Organization.\n        *   Date of Issue.\n        *   Content: Clearly and concisely state the necessary information (What, Who, When, Where, Why - if applicable). Use factual language.\n        *   Contact Person/Information (if further details needed).\n    *   **Format:** Often displayed publicly (e.g., on noticeboards). Should be easy to read and understand quickly. Use of bullet points, bolding, or clear formatting can help.\n\n3.  **Invitations:**\n    *   **Purpose:** To request someone's presence at an event (e.g., party, wedding, meeting, ceremony).\n    *   **Key Elements (often remembered by WWWWWH):**\n        *   Who is inviting? (Host)\n        *   Who is invited? (Guest - often on envelope or addressed in body)\n        *   What is the event?\n        *   Where will it take place?\n        *   When will it take place? (Date and Time)\n        *   (Optional) Why is the event being held? (e.g., birthday, anniversary)\n        *   RSVP information (Répondez s'il vous plaît - Please reply): How and by when to respond.\n        *   Dress code (if applicable).\n    *   **Tone and Format:** Varies depending on formality.\n        *   Formal invitations: Often third-person wording, elegant design, may include separate RSVP card.\n        *   Informal invitations: Can be handwritten, email, or digital; more casual language.\n\n*(Suggested insertion: Sample layouts for a simple advertisement, a formal notice, and a formal invitation, highlighting key information components.)*",
