          "title": "10.1 Skimming, Scanning, Prediction, Inference",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/355952/pexels-photo-355952.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zzxj-mmVO6c",
          "content": "Introduction:\nReading is a complex cognitive process of decoding symbols to derive meaning. Effective reading is not just about recognizing words, but about understanding, interpreting, and engaging with the text. Developing strong reading skills and strategies is crucial for academic success, professional development, and lifelong learning. This chapter will explore various reading techniques, strategies for identifying main ideas and supporting details, approaches to different text types, and the importance of critical reading.\n\n10.1 Skimming, Scanning, Prediction, Inference\n\n**Active Reading vs. Passive Reading:**\n*   Passive reading: Simply letting your eyes move over the words without much engagement or thought.\n*   Active reading: Engaging with the text by questioning, predicting, connecting ideas, summarizing, and evaluating. This leads to better comprehension and retention.\n\n**Reading Strategies:**\nDifferent reading purposes require different strategies.\n1.  **Skimming:**\n    *   **Purpose:** To get a general overview or gist of a text quickly, without reading every word. Useful for deciding if a text is relevant, getting a sense of the main topics, or reviewing material.\n    *   **Technique:** Read the title, headings, subheadings, introduction, conclusion, first and last sentences of paragraphs, look at any visuals (pictures, graphs). Let your eyes move quickly over the text, looking for keywords and main ideas.\n    *   Focus is on speed and overall understanding, not detail.\n\n2.  **Scanning:**\n    *   **Purpose:** To find specific pieces of information within a text quickly (e.g., a name, date, statistic, specific word, answer to a particular question). You already know what you're looking for.\n    *   **Technique:** Move your eyes rapidly down the page or across lines, looking for keywords or specific data related to what you need. You are not trying to understand the whole text. Once you find the relevant information, you may slow down to read that section more carefully.\n    *   Effective scanning requires knowing what you are searching for.\n\n3.  **Prediction:**\n    *   **Purpose:** To anticipate what the text might be about, what will happen next, or what information it will contain, based on clues before and during reading.\n    *   **Technique:**\n        *   *Before Reading:* Use title, headings, cover, illustrations, author information, or prior knowledge to make initial predictions.\n        *   *During Reading:* Continuously make and revise predictions based on what you have read so far. Ask yourself: \"What will happen next?\" \"What does this imply?\"\n    *   Prediction keeps the reader actively engaged and improves comprehension by setting up expectations.\n\n4.  **Inference (Reading Between the Lines):**\n    *   **Purpose:** To understand information that is not explicitly stated in the text but is implied or suggested by the author. It involves drawing conclusions based on evidence in the text combined with your own background knowledge and reasoning.\n    *   **Technique:** Look for clues in the author's word choice, tone, descriptions, actions of characters, and stated facts. Ask questions like: \"What does the author mean by this?\" \"Why did the character do that?\" \"What can I conclude from these details?\"\n    *   Distinguish inference from guessing; inferences must be supported by evidence from the text.\n    *   *Example:* If a text says, \"The sky was dark grey, and the wind howled,\" you can infer it might rain or a storm is approaching, even if it's not directly stated.\n\n*(Suggested insertion: A table comparing skimming and scanning (purpose, technique, speed). Examples of textual clues for making predictions and inferences.)*",
