          "title": "11.2 Comprehension Questions and Contextual Vocabulary",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/3656855/pexels-photo-3656855.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qt36hNDVo90",
          "content": "11.2 Comprehension Questions and Contextual Vocabulary\n\nReading comprehension in literature involves understanding both the literal meaning and the implied or deeper meanings of a text.\n*   **Literal Comprehension:** Understanding what is explicitly stated in the text (e.g., facts, events, main ideas).\n*   **Inferential Comprehension:** Understanding what is implied but not directly stated (making inferences about characters' motivations, themes, author's purpose, etc.).\n*   **Evaluative Comprehension:** Forming judgments about the text (e.g., effectiveness of author's style, validity of arguments, significance of themes).\n\n**Types of Comprehension Questions (Commonly found in literature study):**\n1.  **Factual/Literal Questions:** Test understanding of information directly stated. (Who? What? Where? When?)\n2.  **Inferential Questions:** Require reading between the lines. (Why did a character do something? What can you conclude about...? What does this suggest...?)\n3.  **Vocabulary in Context Questions:** Ask for the meaning of a specific word or phrase as it is used in the passage.\n    *   **Strategy:** Use context clues (surrounding words, sentences, overall meaning of the passage) to infer the meaning. Consider denotation (literal meaning) and connotation (emotional associations). Dictionaries are useful, but understanding context is key.\n4.  **Questions about Literary Elements:** Ask about plot, characterization, setting, theme, point of view, style, tone, use of figurative language, symbolism. (e.g., \"What is the main conflict?\" \"Describe the protagonist's personality.\" \"What is a central theme of the story?\").\n5.  **Questions about Author's Purpose or Intent:** (e.g., \"Why do you think the author included this scene?\").\n6.  **Questions requiring Summarization or Paraphrasing.**\n7.  **Questions asking for Personal Response or Connection (less common in strict comprehension, more in literary discussion):** (e.g., \"How did this story make you feel?\" \"Can you relate to this character's experience?\").\n\n**Strategies for Answering Comprehension Questions:**\n*   Read the passage carefully first to get a general understanding.\n*   Read the questions, then reread relevant parts of the passage to find answers or supporting evidence.\n*   For inferential questions, look for textual clues and use logical reasoning.\n*   For vocabulary questions, try to substitute the answer choices into the sentence to see which makes the most sense in context.\n*   Formulate answers in your own words as much as possible (unless asked to quote).\n*   Ensure answers are complete and directly address the question asked.\n*   Support your interpretations with evidence from the text.\n\n**Dealing with Contextual Vocabulary:**\n*   Identify unfamiliar words.\n*   Look for **context clues**:\n    *   *Synonyms or Definitions:* The meaning might be explained directly or indirectly nearby.\n    *   *Antonyms or Contrast:* The word might be contrasted with another word of opposite meaning.\n    *   *Examples:* The text might provide examples that help clarify the meaning.\n    *   *General Sense of the Passage:* The overall meaning can help you infer the word's meaning.\n    *   *Word Structure (Morphology):* Break down the word into root, prefixes, suffixes if possible (see Ch. 4 & 13).\n*   Use a dictionary if context clues are insufficient or to confirm your inference.",
