          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BezDdLTFEKs",
          "content": "12.4 Punctuation and Capitalization Rules (Brief Review with Emphasis)\n\n(Review from Chapter 3.3, reinforcing key rules for more advanced sentence construction).\n*   **Commas:**\n    *   Separating independent clauses with a coordinating conjunction.\n    *   Setting off introductory elements (phrases, clauses).\n    *   Setting off non-restrictive elements.\n    *   Separating items in a series.\n    *   (Avoid comma splices!).\n*   **Semicolons:**\n    *   Joining closely related independent clauses.\n    *   Separating items in a complex series (that already contain commas).\n*   **Colons:**\n    *   Introducing lists, explanations, quotations.\n*   **Apostrophes:**\n    *   Possessives, contractions. (Common error: it's vs. its).\n*   **Quotation Marks:**\n    *   Direct speech, quoted material. Punctuation relative to closing quotation marks (e.g., commas and periods usually inside in AmE; varies in BrE).\n*   **Parentheses and Dashes:** Setting off parenthetical or amplifying information.\n*   **Capitalization:**\n    *   Start of sentences.\n    *   Proper nouns (names, places, days, months, holidays, titles, brands).\n    *   The pronoun 'I'.\n    *   Major words in titles of works.\n*   Mastery of these conventions is vital for clarity and correctness in written English.",
